Why the design uses agent_id as the logical model name (and the rejected
alternatives):

The user's earlier diagnostic agent identified three real bugs (server
LlmRouter was global-only; AgentLlmConfig had no model field; add_agent
copied the global config). The user then asked for a model-name-as-
indirection approach: harness uses {agent_id} as its model name; server
resolves to real (provider, model). I considered three layouts:

1. {agent_id}_model{num} (the user's literal suggestion). Reserves room
   for multiple models per agent. Skipped — every code path has exactly
   one current model per agent today; the {num} suffix would be dead
   structure. Trivial to add later by changing one bind_agent() call.

2. {agent_id}-real-model passthrough — set LLM_MODEL to a real model name
   and have the router only resolve real names. Skipped because it
   requires restarting the container to swap the model (e.g. budget
   degradation), defeating the indirection's main reason to exist. The
   existing budget code already intercepts request.model in the proxy,
   but the indirection in the router lets us also serve callers that
   don't go through the budget path.

3. {agent_id} (chosen). Single hop: harness sends body.model=atlas, the
   router has bind atlas->("openai|key|", "gpt-4o"), chat() rewrites
   the request and dispatches. Both /v1/chat/completions and /v1/messages
   resolve through the same map.

Critical tripwire: the Claude SDK harness has the line
  use_model = model if model != AGENT_NAME else LLM_MODEL
at harnesses/claude-sdk/harness.py:265. Setting LLM_MODEL=AGENT_NAME
makes both branches yield AGENT_NAME, which is fine — the server
resolves at the end. But it means any change to that substitution rule
needs to keep ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL pointed at the xpressclaw server (not
api.anthropic.com), or the SDK will send the unrecognized agent_id
straight to Anthropic and get a 400.

Provider-instance dedup key is provider_type|api_key|base_url. Two
agents on the same OpenAI key share one OpenAiProvider; two on different
keys get separate ones. This was the previous PR's silent bug — it
generated unique instance names per agent then prefix-matched on
HashMap iteration order, so requests routed non-deterministically.

provider=local (embedded llama.cpp) is back. The previous PR removed
the registration path entirely; this one re-adds it, gated on
#[cfg(feature = "local-llm")] and requires model_path. The
post-GGUF-download flow now patches llm.model_path into every
provider=local agent rather than touching a removed global field.

Container env scrubbed of real cloud keys. The previous code passed
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY/OPENAI_API_KEY into the container if the agent or
global config had them. Now the container only ever sees placeholder
sk-ant-<agent_id> / sk-xpressclaw-<agent_id> keys. The proxy uses these
placeholders to identify the agent and pulls the real key from server-
side config. test_build_container_spec_no_real_keys_in_container locks
this in — if a future change leaks the key, the test fails.

env_overrides now per-agent. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY env applies only to
agents with provider=anthropic; OPENAI_API_KEY/OPENAI_BASE_URL only to
provider=openai. Previously these clobbered global fields that no longer
exist.

Backward compat: old YAMLs with top-level agent.model still load —
Config::migrate_legacy_fields() moves it into llm.model. Old YAMLs with
the dropped global llm fields (default_provider, openai_api_key, etc.)
load fine because serde ignores unknown fields when there's no
deny_unknown_fields. The DEFAULT_CONFIG_TEMPLATE now writes the new
shape.

Routes that mutate agents (add_agent, update_agent_config) now rebuild
the router via LlmRouter::build_from_config. Reusing the old router on a
config edit was a latent bug — the new agent's binding wouldn't exist
until next server restart.

Tests cover six scenarios:
- migrate_legacy_top_level_model + does_not_overwrite_explicit_llm_model
- effective_model fallback ordering
- router resolves logical agent name
- router resolves real model name (for direct callers)
- two agents share one provider instance when keys+url match
- set_agent_model re-points an existing binding
- container spec doesn't leak real api_key into env

Not included in this PR: deleting the unrelated buck-out/ tree and
external/ submodule drift; those weren't introduced here.
